[Music]  this morning a return visit from a  master of meat  pat lefreda last appeared on this  broadcast in  2014. since then he's further expanded  his empire now he's out with a new book  glorious beef a mix of personal stories  recipes in the state of his  ever-changing industry  to sample all of it we visited his  one-of-a-kind brand new building just  outside new york city  how big is this  it's 120 feet deep  30 feet wide  30 feet tall  inside pat lafrida's new 20 million  dollar facility in new jersey  is the fort knox of food  the world's largest dry aging room where  millions of dollars worth of meat  up to 15 000 primal cuts  sit for up to 260 days so it should lose  about 15 to 20 percent of its water  weight in the first 21 days and then it  starts to slow down as they lose weight  they concentrate flavor you'll see that  the protein is starting to bevel in  but the bone won't recede the fat won't  recede why is dry aging so important  dry aging is is an art that  adds so much richness and flavor to beef  but it has to be done correctly you have  a lot of fronts  no one does dry aging like lefrida who  runs a family business that first got  its start a century ago  in his new book glorious beef lefreeda  celebrates his family's legacy but also  gets personal talking about his battle  with stomach cancer in 2003 which he  says led him to stop eating processed  meat  he also talks about his service in the  army it shaped me in a way which no  other experience can i mean to be that  regimented to learn how to live through  and thrive through sleep deprivation  was the first thing i thought  you put you pulled an all-nighter last  night i did  and you do it all the time yes  without sleeping no matter what happens  food must go out  since march 16th of 2020 like all meat  production facilities lefreeda meat  purveyors has been under a presidential  mandate never to close now look at my  staff  they're not under presidential mandate  they could have stayed home but they  didn't i owe a lot of that to the army  team building  leadership  and communication a butcher's knife  always has a curved blade despite the  lack of sleep lefreda was still game to  give us a brief butchering demonstration  making one of his favorites skirt steak  pinwheels  once we got the meat trimmed and  seasoned it was time to tie it up  the reason that we tie meat  is so that the density is as symmetrical  as possible because when it's not it  will not cook evenly  so  i'm going to do a butcher's knot real  slow  and you follow along with me okay okay  all right  see i'm holding it yeah  now i'm taking this end yeah i'm going  right over my thumb okay  under  how the hell do you do that  we're gonna do it again  you know what's funny the slower you do  it the harder it is i feel like i'm  teaching my son how to tie his shoes i  want you to he's yelling at you what do  you hold on to only i'm yelling at you  now you're almost there all you need to  know is that i got there eventually  that's it that's it you got it you gotta  look at that perfect  kind of  you do it you do it i'm gonna do it you  did it you did it  the next step was to split the wheel  right down the middle that looks great  yours is going to look just as good you  want you just put yours  beautiful  split it right out right in half  that's  yeah it is beautiful  lefreda finished up the pinwheels in a  makeshift kitchen on the back of his  truck in the alley behind his building  he says the secret here is to cook from  the top down  not the bottom up  there's the tailgate barbecue and then  there's the back alley barbecue  that's the way we do it  are you ready to taste i am ready  creation i'm ready  our personal creation  butchered  cooked  and eaten  by us cheers great job  oh  nothing better glorious beef however is  more than a collection of recipes and  personal stories what always bothered me  was the misinformation about meat i  wanted to go back to a time where i  remembered beef was glorious and that's  where we get the title of the book  glorious beef lefrieda uses the book to  push back against trends he believes  aren't always smart like exclusively  grass fed beef which he says costs too  much and simply doesn't taste as good  i'm not against grass-fed it's just  you'll never get that same sweetness it  only comes from finishing off on green  it's a part of the ongoing organic  versus all natural debate one highly  regulated the other not  neither animals are raised with growth  hormones or antibiotics organic  is one step further where the lands have  to be certified organic you're not a fan  of organic i'm a fan of  of organic i can't justify the costs  organic is probably about 20 to 30  percent higher in cost than all natural  prices fluctuate labels and production  standards evolve but one thing stays the  same good beef is good beef  and for lefreda  that's been a lifetime labor of love  you haven't met too many wealthy  butchers  it's hard work  and one of the reasons that my dad never  wanted me in this business is because  of he didn't want me to have to work  overnight he didn't want me to have to  work in the cold but  it's in the blood what about your kids  i don't want them to suffer through this  either do they want to  they they talk about it i mean i think  my daughter at 11 is more of a fighter  than my son he's more of a lover at 17.  but she reminds me a lot of of my aunt  who was my dad's partner  and it takes a strong personality to to  make it in this industry do you ever see  yourself not doing this  i've never seen myself not doing this  and courtesy of pat lafrida who dropped  these off this morning after cooking  oh my god  personally for you guys engraved look at  that tomahawk steaks that are each about  two feet long i mean this is we have  people taking pictures  my son is eyeballing this like right now  he's like mom get down to philly with  that well it's free you can throw it  back on the grill when you get home i'm  going to eat something i'm not going to  share any with one sean brandy or jack  grand even i took their last name for  this one i mean it smell i mean it  tastes amazing too but this smells so  good i just love that pat  is constantly reinvesting in the  business yeah  like anybody he makes just throws back  in wow to get better at what he does  anyway i will take a bite thank you pat  for dropping yeah i will take a bite in  a moment i promise

